{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>He made him ride on the high places of the earth.</b> As it says elsewhere: ״ואתה על במותימו תדרוך״." "<b>He ate the produce of the field.</b> Once they came to the land they ate the produce of the field and the manna ceased, as it is written: ״וישבות המן ממחרת הפסח ויאכלו מעבור הארץ״ — so that they would grow strong to conquer the land. And we say there that they first brought the omer and then ate. The Rabbi Nathan ha-Ofeniel raised the question: why did they wait until after the bringing of the omer? Let the positive commandment of ״בערב תאכלו מצות״ come and push aside the negative commandment of ״ולחם וקלי וכרמל״ etc., just as the act of circumcision pushed aside the negative commandment of tzara'at. He answered: it is a decree — a first olive-volume lest [one come to eat] a second olive-volume. For by the first olive-volume that he would eat of matzah he would fulfill the positive commandment, and would then transgress the prohibition with the second olive-volume. In such a manner we say in the case of a yevamah who fell before a Kohen Gadol: let the positive commandment of yibum come — ״יבמה יבא עליה״ — and push aside the negative commandment of ״אלמנה לא יקחו״, and we answer: it is a decree — a first act of relations lest [there be] a second act of relations, for by the first act of relations he would fulfill the positive commandment. Here too, it is the same." "<b>He nursed him with honey from the rock.</b> For the bees made honey in the crevices of the rocks. And also the dates grow on the rocks and honey comes out." "<b>And oil from the flinty rock.</b> For the olives that grow in the rocks are better than those in the valley, because the sun shines on them constantly and sweetens them, as it says elsewhere: ״וממגד תבואות שמש״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:49.450609797Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}